import{_ as t,o as e,c as a,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.CqpfiAoK.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Continental European Social Civilization","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/34-欧洲大陆社会型文明.md","filePath":"en/34-欧洲大陆社会型文明.md"}'),o={name:"en/34-欧洲大陆社会型文明.md"};function l(s,i,r,c,u,d){return e(),a("div",null,[...i[0]||(i[0]=[n('<h1 id="continental-european-social-civilization" tabindex="-1">Continental European Social Civilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#continental-european-social-civilization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Continental European Social Civilization&quot;">​</a></h1><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Document No.</td><td>34</td></tr><tr><td>Module</td><td>Civilization Typologies and Social Systems</td></tr><tr><td>Keywords</td><td>Continental European Social, Social Market, Co-Determination of Labor and Capital, Consensus Democracy, Balanced Development</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="i-origins-and-core-concepts-of-the-continental-european-social-civilization" tabindex="-1">I. Origins and Core Concepts of the Continental European Social Civilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#i-origins-and-core-concepts-of-the-continental-european-social-civilization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;I. Origins and Core Concepts of the Continental European Social Civilization&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Continental European Social Civilization is the most balanced of the five symbiotic civilization typologies, with origins traceable to the social models of continental European countries in the real world (mainly Germany, France, and other Western European countries). Through long-term development, continental European countries formed the &quot;social market economy&quot; model, emphasizing the balance between market efficiency and social equity, achieving a harmonious coexistence of economic development and social stability. After the establishment of the Anchor Symbiotic Civilization, this model combined with symbiotic concepts and was upgraded to create the unique civilizational form of the &quot;Continental European Social Civilization.&quot;</p><p>The core concept of the Continental European Social Civilization can be summarized in eight characters: &quot;social market, co-determination of labor and capital.&quot; Specifically:</p><ol><li><strong>Social market economy</strong>: on the one hand, the market plays a fundamental role in resource allocation, ensuring efficiency; on the other hand, the government uses social policies to correct market failures, ensuring fairness. &quot;As much market as possible, as much state as necessary&quot;</li><li><strong>Co-determination of labor and capital</strong>: in the relationship between labor and capital (in the cultivation field, between ordinary cultivators and resource holders, management institutions), the civilization emphasizes equal dialogue and joint decision-making, avoiding the excessive dominance of either side</li><li><strong>Consensus democracy</strong>: in the field of social governance, the civilization emphasizes consultation, dialogue, and consensus, pursuing &quot;win-win&quot; rather than &quot;zero-sum&quot;</li><li><strong>Balanced development</strong>: the civilization pursues the overall balance of economy, society, culture, and environment, rather than one-sided development</li></ol><p>These concepts of the Continental European Social Civilization are highly compatible with the core concepts of the Anchor Symbiotic Civilization: &quot;common progress of the Myriad Races&quot; emphasizes the common development of all races, &quot;More Work, More Reward&quot; emphasizes the fairness of distribution, and &quot;A Path for Everyone&quot; emphasizes the guarantee of everyone&#39;s development rights.</p><h2 id="ii-the-social-structure-of-the-continental-european-social-civilization" tabindex="-1">II. The Social Structure of the Continental European Social Civilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#ii-the-social-structure-of-the-continental-european-social-civilization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;II. The Social Structure of the Continental European Social Civilization&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The social structure of the Continental European Social Civilization takes &quot;medium government + strong associations + market + social dialogue&quot; as its basic features:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Structural Element</th><th>Feature Description</th><th>Core Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Medium Government</td><td>The government has medium scale and functions, responsible for macro regulation and social welfare</td><td>Providing institutional framework and social protection</td></tr><tr><td>Strong Associations</td><td>Professional associations, trade unions, and employer organizations are well developed</td><td>Organizing social consultation, maintaining professional order</td></tr><tr><td>Market Mechanism</td><td>The market plays a fundamental role in resource allocation</td><td>Ensuring allocation efficiency</td></tr><tr><td>Social Dialogue</td><td>Tripartite consultation mechanism among government, associations, and market subjects</td><td>Coordinating interests, achieving consensus</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="_2-1-medium-government" tabindex="-1">2.1 Medium Government <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-1-medium-government" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2.1 Medium Government&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The government of the Continental European Social Civilization is a typical &quot;social state,&quot; with medium scale and functions:</p><ul><li>Maintaining macro social stability and public order</li><li>Providing social welfare such as education, medical care, housing, and pensions</li><li>Regulating the market, preventing excessive market concentration and unfair competition</li><li>Organizing social dialogue, coordinating the interests of all parties</li></ul><p>The core concept of the medium government is &quot;subsidiarity&quot; — the government should not do what society can do itself; society should not do what individuals can do themselves; the government only does what society and individuals cannot do.</p><h3 id="_2-2-strong-associations" tabindex="-1">2.2 Strong Associations <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-2-strong-associations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2.2 Strong Associations&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The Continental European Social Civilization has well-developed professional associations and social organizations:</p><ul><li><strong>Trade unions</strong>: representing the interests of ordinary cultivators, negotiating with resource holders and management institutions</li><li><strong>Employer organizations</strong>: representing the interests of resource holders and management institutions</li><li><strong>Professional associations</strong>: professional organizations of various cultivation directions, responsible for industry standards and professional certification</li><li><strong>Cooperatives</strong>: cooperative economic organizations voluntarily formed by cultivators</li></ul><p>These associations play a significant role in social life, forming a social governance pattern of &quot;state—associations—individuals&quot; with three levels of dialogue.</p><h3 id="_2-3-social-dialogue" tabindex="-1">2.3 Social Dialogue <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-3-social-dialogue" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2.3 Social Dialogue&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Social dialogue is the core governance mechanism of the Continental European Social Civilization:</p><ul><li><strong>Tripartite consultation</strong>: the government, cultivators&#39; organizations, and resource holders&#39; organizations hold regular tripartite consultations on important social and economic matters</li><li><strong>Collective bargaining</strong>: cultivators&#39; organizations and resource holders&#39; organizations negotiate on resource allocation, cultivation conditions, and advancement standards</li><li><strong>Work council</strong>: within communities and sects, work councils composed of cultivators&#39; representatives and management representatives jointly decide on internal affairs</li></ul><p>Through social dialogue, the Continental European Social Civilization coordinates the interests of all parties, achieving &quot;win-win&quot; rather than &quot;zero-sum.&quot;</p><h2 id="iii-the-cultivation-model-of-the-continental-european-social-civilization" tabindex="-1">III. The Cultivation Model of the Continental European Social Civilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#iii-the-cultivation-model-of-the-continental-european-social-civilization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;III. The Cultivation Model of the Continental European Social Civilization&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The cultivation model of the Continental European Social Civilization takes &quot;dual-track, cooperative, balanced&quot; as its core features:</p><h3 id="_3-1-dual-track-cultivation-system" tabindex="-1">3.1 Dual-Track Cultivation System <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-1-dual-track-cultivation-system" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.1 Dual-Track Cultivation System&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The Continental European Social Civilization implements a dual-track cultivation system:</p><ul><li><strong>Market track</strong>: cultivating cultivation talents according to market demand through market-oriented cultivation institutions and sects</li><li><strong>Social track</strong>: providing universal cultivation education and training through public cultivation institutions, ensuring every citizen&#39;s right to cultivate</li></ul><p>The two tracks complement each other: the market track ensures cultivation efficiency and industry demand, while the social track ensures cultivation fairness and universal coverage.</p><h3 id="_3-2-school-enterprise-cooperation" tabindex="-1">3.2 School-Enterprise Cooperation <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-2-school-enterprise-cooperation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.2 School-Enterprise Cooperation&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The Continental European Social Civilization emphasizes the cooperation between cultivation academies and sects/enterprises:</p><ul><li><strong>Academy-enterprise co-construction</strong>: academies and sects jointly develop cultivation curricula, jointly train talents, and jointly conduct research</li><li><strong>Apprenticeship system</strong>: cultivators combine cultivation academy learning with sect practice, with theory and practice interwoven</li><li><strong>Talent rotation</strong>: talents flow between academies and sects/enterprises, promoting knowledge exchange</li></ul><p>School-enterprise cooperation closely combines cultivation education with practical needs, improving the pertinence and efficiency of talent cultivation.</p><h3 id="_3-3-balanced-cultivation" tabindex="-1">3.3 Balanced Cultivation <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-3-balanced-cultivation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.3 Balanced Cultivation&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The Continental European Social Civilization emphasizes balanced cultivation:</p><ul><li><strong>Balance between theory and practice</strong>: not only cultivation realm but also theoretical knowledge and practical ability</li><li><strong>Balance between cultivation and life</strong>: cultivators have sufficient rest and family time, avoiding overwork</li><li><strong>Balance between individual and society</strong>: individual development is integrated into social development</li></ul><p>This balanced cultivation model cultivates &quot;fully developed cultivators&quot; rather than &quot;cultivation machines.&quot;</p><h2 id="iv-the-output-linked-contract-mechanism-of-the-continental-european-social-civilization" tabindex="-1">IV. The Output-Linked Contract Mechanism of the Continental European Social Civilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#iv-the-output-linked-contract-mechanism-of-the-continental-european-social-civilization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;IV. The Output-Linked Contract Mechanism of the Continental European Social Civilization&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The output-linked contract mechanism of the Continental European Social Civilization takes &quot;social partnership contracting&quot; as its main form:</p><ol><li><strong>Multi-party participation</strong>: cultivators, mentors, and management institutions jointly participate in contract signing and implementation</li><li><strong>Consultative decision</strong>: resource allocation plans are determined through consultation among all parties, rather than unilateral decisions</li><li><strong>Interest balance</strong>: contracting design emphasizes the balance of interests of all parties, avoiding excessive concentration of benefits</li><li><strong>Social responsibility</strong>: contracting parties bear certain social responsibilities, such as providing cultivation opportunities for new cultivators and supporting community public welfare</li></ol><p>The advantage of this &quot;social partnership contracting&quot; mechanism is its ability to coordinate the interests of all parties, achieving social harmony and stable development; it also enhances the social responsibility and sense of belonging of all parties.</p><h2 id="v-advantages-and-challenges-of-the-continental-european-social-civilization" tabindex="-1">V. Advantages and Challenges of the Continental European Social Civilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#v-advantages-and-challenges-of-the-continental-european-social-civilization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;V. Advantages and Challenges of the Continental European Social Civilization&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="_5-1-typical-advantages" tabindex="-1">5.1 Typical Advantages <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-1-typical-advantages" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5.1 Typical Advantages&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Strong stability</strong>: the social dialogue mechanism effectively resolves social conflicts, maintaining long-term social stability</li><li><strong>High cohesion</strong>: consensus culture and social partnership enhance social cohesion</li><li><strong>Harmonious labor-capital relations</strong>: the co-determination of labor and capital mechanism avoids sharp confrontation between labor and capital</li><li><strong>Balanced development</strong>: the balanced development concept avoids one-sidedness and ensures comprehensive development</li></ul><h3 id="_5-2-facing-challenges" tabindex="-1">5.2 Facing Challenges <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-2-facing-challenges" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5.2 Facing Challenges&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Lower administrative efficiency</strong>: overemphasis on consultation and dialogue may slow down decision-making</li><li><strong>Higher institutional costs</strong>: complex consultation mechanisms and social welfare systems bring higher institutional operating costs</li><li><strong>Reform resistance</strong>: established interest patterns may resist reform, affecting the adaptability of the system</li><li><strong>External competitiveness</strong>: the pursuit of consensus may lack the decisiveness and aggressiveness of other typologies in external competition</li></ul><p>In response to these challenges, the Continental European Social Civilization is actively absorbing the advantages of other typologies, such as introducing the efficiency mechanism of the East Asian Intensive Type and the innovation incentive mechanism of the North American Pluralistic Type, improving development efficiency while maintaining social characteristics.</p><h2 id="conclusion" tabindex="-1">Conclusion <a class="header-anchor" href="#conclusion" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Conclusion&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Continental European Social Civilization is the most balanced civilization typology of the Anchor Symbiotic Civilization. With the core concept of &quot;social market, co-determination of labor and capital,&quot; it achieves stable development and social harmony through the social structure of medium government, strong associations, market mechanism, and social dialogue, the dual-track cooperative cultivation model, and the social partnership contracting output-link mechanism. While maintaining its own characteristics, the Continental European Social Civilization is also continuously exploring the best balance between market and society, efficiency and fairness.</p><h2 id="related-documents" tabindex="-1">Related Documents <a class="header-anchor" href="#related-documents" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related Documents&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>30-五种共生文明分型总览 (Overview of the Five Symbiotic Civilization Typologies)</li><li>33-北美多元型文明 (North American Pluralistic Civilization)</li><li>36-社会结构与阶层流动 (Social Structure and Class Mobility)</li></ul>',50)])])}const m=t(o,[["render",l]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
